==English==

===Etymology===
The word ''osmolarity'' ( [https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/osmolarity /ˌäzməˈlerədē/]) uses [[wikipedia:Classical_compound|combining forms]] of [[wikipedia:Osmosis|osmotic, osmosis]], molar[[wikipedia:Molar_concentration|<small>[1]</small>]][[mwod:molar|<small>[2]</small>]] and [https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/-ity -ity]. [https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/osmolarity <small><nowiki>[3]</nowiki></small>] Osmotic and osmosis are [[Latin]] from [[Greek]]: ˈɒzməʊs ''ōsmos'', "a push".[https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/osmose <small><nowiki>[4]</nowiki></small>][https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/osmosis <small><nowiki>[5]</nowiki></small>] Molar is [[Middle English]] from [[Latin]]: ''molares'' and ''molaris'', "grinding, mill".[[mwod:molar|<small>[6]</small>]][[molar#English|<small>[7]</small>]] The -ity suffix is from [[Middle French]] and [[Latin]]: ɪti, ''-ité'' and ''-itas, -itatis'', "quality, state, degree".[https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/-ity <small><nowiki>[8]</nowiki></small>][[mwod:-ity|<small>[9]</small>]]. This combination of words yields the idea of "a push to measure the quality of moles per [[solute]]", referencing [[chemistry]].[[wikipedia:Molar_concentration#Definition|<small>[1]</small>]][https://www.lexico.com/en/definition/osmosis <small><nowiki>[5]</nowiki></small>][[mwod:-ity|<small>[9]</small>]]{{rfe|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|osmolarities}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry}} The [[osmotic]] [[concentration]] of a [[solution]], normally expressed as [[osmole]]s of [[solute]] per [[litre]] of [[solution]].

